THE FINAL COREGRID UI LAW
The entire engine can be reduced to ten laws:
LAW 1 — Reality before interface
The UI reflects the underlying system reality.
LAW 2 — Intent before navigation
The system should understand what the user is trying to accomplish.
LAW 3 — Context before composition
The workspace is generated from context.
LAW 4 — Semantics before aesthetics
A component's meaning comes before its appearance.
LAW 5 — State must be observable
Important system states cannot be hidden.
LAW 6 — Intelligence must be distinguishable
Human, AI, automated, inferred, predicted and simulated information must not be deceptively conflated.
LAW 7 — Autonomy must be bounded
Agents require explicit permissions, constraints and escalation mechanisms.
LAW 8 — Every important action must be reversible where technically possible
Undo, rollback, versioning and auditability are first-class UX primitives.
LAW 9 — The interface adapts; the truth does not
Presentation can change according to context, but underlying facts and permissions cannot be visually rewritten by personalization.
LAW 10 — The interface disappears when it becomes unnecessary
The ultimate CoreGrid interface is not the most visually impressive interface.
It is the interface that allows intelligence, humans, agents and reality to interact with the least unnecessary friction.

